husiastic and shipped  a few extra samples  um so you're on to the lab sample  management system then  probably a limb system and then it's  analyzed probably on the same system  data is transferred and reported and  finally  samples if it's a pk sample will  probably be destroyed after  a final report or it may be shipped back  to the the  originator to the site if it's a tumor  sample in particular  um the investigators they're clearly  very precious samples  investigators like them back um so  they'll be  returned to the investigator or if the  protocol  has given consent um it they could be  put into long-term storage for future  particularly by marker  examinations now i would say we have one  step  more to go on this um and that's at the  the kip stage so your kit  is where the sample ids your unique  sample ids  are present that are going to help that  whole  life cycle process and all of that  can be managed by your limbs so this  entire process  can be managed by your limbs if you have  the the correct limb size  i just have one more um thing to say  about labeling because  um it it's such a small thing but you  know it's absolutely  critical something goes wrong in your  label you have lost that sample  so a unique identifier is  um really important and that's often  linked to the barcode if you're using  barcodes  some clients don't particularly want to  use a barcode  excuse me um i think they're really  valuable things to have  even if your lab limbs is  a disconnected um system from your own  if you've got a bar code that can be  uploaded onto the lab limbs  um system no problem so it helps with  that  seamless transition and you can use one  or two d  bar chords and i i guess that's um  really up to personal preference calibri  we tend to use 2d because you can get  more  human readable um information on the  label  just from physically fitting and as  the bioanalyst in me that's really  useful to have  that's the last thing i'm going to  lecture on labeling  but those two items here these are  absolutely  um i would say crucial um to  maintain um your your sample tracking  so finally we get on to how  limbs are set up and i've  tried to separate it out into manual  processes  and what's managed either in a  completely automated way  or a semi-automated way um through the  limbs um so first thing we do when we  get a  um study in is create a sample upload  file  for a patient so each patient you know  exactly what samples  you're going to expect and that file is  created  no sample ids at all and that file is  uploaded  every time it's uploaded into the lim  system for a new patient  it will automatically create the sample  id  um automatically associate that with the  bar  code and that enables you to then select  the samples that you want to print and  print the labels  and make the kits um now some limbs  our olm system does allow um  the tracking of the kit inventory which  is really very useful  um but i won't go into that too much  because that's obviously not essential  for  um the regulatory aspects  so you ship your kits and the sample is  taken  and then i've got here location updated  and i'm going to come back to that in  the next slide  because that's a really key point of  when you see the visibility of that  sample  when is your limb system updated to say  hello this sample is no longer an empty  tube  it's got blood or plasma in it  and then from that you can prepare a  manifest and arrange  for the shipments they'll be the receipt  remember very important to receive your  samples um  and again up up  because it's now sitting at the lab it's  no longer sitting  at the clinic a little bit of  reconciliation here i've got that as a  manual process  i unfortunately haven't found a way to  automate that i would love to  and then analysis and reporting as well  can be done on the limbs so that is  pretty much  all of your process um by that initial  upload  and i've just put in here check consent  um  so this is where you can  customize your limbs so that for  particular samples if they're not  mandated samples  that you can have a checkbox um and and  just  make sure that that sample you've  received the patient has consented on it  and that will keep you straight with jcp  so going back to this locations and when  you actually see the visibility  of the sample um so there are two kind  of main  ways um that  the whole trial works one is to ship to  a central lab and this the samples are  then distributed from there  and the other is to ship directly from  um the the clinical site to the  laboratory um  so if we look at them then the the first  one you've got your limbs is  exactly i'm just going to come a little  bit closer to the screen because  my old eyes can't see what i'm doing  here that's better  so the lims is expecting a sample  the sample is collected then it's  shipped to the central facility  at that point that is when  you have visibility that the sample  exists  um and similarly if the samples are  shipped directly you've got your limbs  expecting the samples the samples  collected  and then you need a way of  copying that paperwork to the person  managing  the limbs um and that is  triggered that the the sample has now  been collected  now this time if that's done on the day  that's  fantastic unfortunately very busy  clinics um  it tends to be considerably more than a  day  um and then the limbs is updated  so you know here it's quite late in the  day or could be quite late in the day  before your limbs even knows that the  sample exists  and if you think back gcp or um  gclp um wants you to maintain chain of  custody  and part of that chain of custody starts  from the minute the sample is taken  so we're very lucky that we have a  really good relationship with cloud  limbs  and that's meant that we've been  involved in future developments and  we've been able to contribute  to how a future development could look  and work in the clinical space  and one such development is um  well it's they call it a client portable  portal we use it as a clinic  or lab interface so here and if we go to  stage one  we've got um the limbs expecting the  sample as before  this time the nurse taking the sample  can log in to our limbs from the portal  scan the sample that they're about to  take  and the system will prompt them for uh  two  pieces of information which they would  need to write down anyway  of the manual process and that's patient  id  and the date that it's taken done  that's all they need to do so where  our limbs is got notification from  um real time that that sample  now exists um so that that's  changed um our compliance  uh considerably and then lastly we have  an audit trail really important as i  said at the beginning  not just to understand that a sample was  expected  and then at some point in the line um  it's been taken and disposed of you've  got to know all the points  in between um so on the the top left  here  we have um a short from  a limb system the um each sample  will be an individual line and each one  has this log attached to it and you  simply  click on the log and if we look here  all these entries are for sample cl  one three um i have edited it slightly  we don't um like to put people's names  in presentations so  here we've got mickey mouse has created  uh  the sample that's got being expected on  the 22nd of january  uh this year um the  paperwork is required um but at that  time it hadn't been received the sample  didn't exist  and then we received it now we received  it on the 10th of  the sorry the 7th of april um but you  can see  here that the collection date um was  actually i can't see that the 9th of  march  um 21. so that's nearly a month that  it's taken  um to to get that uh  paperwork and the visibility of the  sample so that's where your  client portal is really very important  but at least we can document that we we  know that that's what's happened  at that time the sample has changed from  pending to  um that it's sitting at the clinical  site and then somewhere later we've got  donald duck has  um arranged for shipment uh on the 12th  of may  and the sample's been shipped from the  site  in this case uh to our own lamps  um and this is being held in this  freezer in this box and if you click on  that again  you'll find out exactly the position of  the box  so you have a full audit trace um trail  that's  very easy to bring back for any sample  so if we just recap then and go back to  what the regulatory expectations are  um so have we managed to address them  all  with their limbs um well  i would say pretty much um everything in  pink  we have managed by our limb system um  the only thing that i would say um a  little question mark on  is the shipping records and i don't mean  have  the um the actual samples been received  we do record that in the limbs  um but we don't currently  use it for courier paperwork so we're  not  would not recording that the box was  received containing samples  and we keep that manually so that's the  only aspect  of our whole sample workflow that isn't  on our lym  system so i hope  um that that's showing you that um it's  it's very easy to set up  manage the whole workflow um  reduce errors reduce that human  um possibility of making a human error  um by increasing the automation um  this is a rather odd situation where  we're recording  um up front um so i would normally say  please feel free to ask me any questions  i'll address there and i'll be more than  delighted  um if you want to uh reach out to me um  at any point in time and we can discuss  your situation so thank you very much
 

